    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in team interactions. Communication: Assist in clear communication of plans and roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve misunderstandings by miming or gesturing roles in a group travel project (e.g., uses gestures to show who is in charge of booking transportation and who is responsible for finding accommodations). Can clarify project plans with role cards or diagrams to show responsibilities (e.g., uses labeled visuals to show each group member's role in coordinating a cultural trip). Can repeat and rephrase using gestures to confirm understanding in multilingual settings (e.g., signals agreement by nodding and confirms details with simple phrases).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to recall an unforgettable travel experience or a unique cultural tradition they've learned about (e.g., "What is one tradition you found interesting while you were traveling?"). Students share in pairs, then a few share with the class, focusing on using vocabulary like 'diversity', 'adventure', 'explore', 'learn'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to rephrase a question or statement using simple gestures to confirm understanding. For example, the teacher might say, "So, if I could travel anywhere, I would visit Egypt. Do you mean the pyramids?" while miming a pyramid shape. The teacher emphasizes vowel reduction in "could" and "would" when asking hypothetical questions: "If I could TRAVEL aROUND the WORLD, I would VISIT EGYPT." and the stress on 'adVENture'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'mediation' and 'clarification'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If I'm confused about my role in a project, do I stay quiet or do I ask for clarification?" (Expected answer: Ask for clarification.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If someone explains something and I don't understand, what can I do to help them explain it better?" (Expected answer: Ask them to rephrase, use gestures, ask specific questions.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short "Project Briefing" document for the 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project, formatted as instructions for a group task. The briefing includes roles (e.g., "Research Coordinator," "Content Creator," "Visual Designer") and initial responsibilities, but with some intentional ambiguities or complex sentences to prompt the need for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Lead group projects that explore cultural diversity and the personal benefits of travel, ensuring everyone has input. Pragmatic: Respectfully discussing cultural differences and travel experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist Question: "What is the main goal of the 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project?" (Expected: To compare cultures using superlatives and discuss travel experiences.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information Matching: Students match potential roles (e.g., 'Research Coordinator') to initial responsibilities described in the briefing (e.g., 'Gathers information on traditions and destinations').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "The Content Creator is only responsible for images." (False – they also write descriptions.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Second conditional, superlatives, past continuous. Vocabulary: 'explore', 'compare', 'experience', 'diversity', 'destination', 'understanding', 'perspectives', 'traditions'. Pronunciation: Vowel reduction in 'could'/'would', stress on 'adVENture', 'des-ti-NA-tion'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Clarification Gap-Fill):</b> Students complete sentences that require clarification or rephrasing, integrating target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "If I could travel anywhere, I ____ visit Egypt. Do you mean the country with the most breathtaking ______?" (would / pyramids). Students also fill in phrases like "Could you explain that again?" or "So, if I understand correctly, ____?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled (Role Card Mediation):</b> In small groups (3-4 students), each student receives a "Role Card" for the 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project (e.g., "Blogger: Writes main content, uses superlatives to compare cultures."). Students take turns explaining their role to the group, intentionally leaving out one detail or using slightly ambiguous language. Others must use mediation phrases (e.g., "Could you rephrase that using simpler words, please?") and gestures to clarify and ensure full understanding. Teacher monitors for accurate grammar and pronunciation (vowel reduction, stress on multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Confirming Project Steps):</b> Students work in pairs. One student describes a hypothetical step in their Cultural Comparison Blog project (e.g., "First, we research the most interesting traditions of different cultures. While traveling, I was learning new cultures through observation."). The partner must repeat and rephrase what they understood, using gestures to confirm (e.g., nodding agreement, miming 'researching'), and ask for any necessary clarification. They practice showing interest: "How was your experience abroad in regards to changing your perspective?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students form their 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project groups (3-4 students). Their task is to collaboratively define and clarify the specific roles and responsibilities for each group member within their blog project. They must create a visual aid (e.g., a simple diagram on a whiteboard, a digital slide, or hand-drawn 'role cards' with responsibilities) that clearly outlines who is responsible for what. One student acts as the group leader (Sociolinguistic: Lead group projects, ensuring everyone has input). The groups then exchange their visual aids with another group. Each group member from the presenting group must verbally explain their role, using gestures and rephrasing as needed to ensure the other group fully understands their plan. They will practice explaining hypothetical situations using the second conditional ("If I were responsible for images, I would find the most breathtaking photos of diverse destinations.") and narrating experiences with the past continuous ("While researching, I was discovering many unique traditions."). The interaction should involve respectfully discussing cultural differences and showing interest in how the other group plans to explore their global community.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project plan, specifically focusing on the clarity of their assigned roles and responsibilities, using their visual aid. They must demonstrate how they would resolve misunderstandings or clarify information if a new group member joined or if there was confusion about a task. The teacher observes and uses an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (accurate stress on multisyllabic words like 'adVENture', 'des-ti-NA-tion', and vowel reduction in 'could'/'would').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'diversity', 'perspectives', 'unforgettable', 'enriching') related to cultures and travel.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanations of roles and responsibilities for the blog project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successful resolution of simulated misunderstandings using rephrasing, gestures, or visual aids.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and equitable participation among group members, demonstrating the ability to lead group projects and ensure everyone has input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Second Conditional and Superlatives when discussing blog content.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did our group use gestures and rephrasing to clarify roles and responsibilities for our Cultural Comparison Blog?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of ensuring everyone in your group understood the project plan, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing mediation skills today help you feel more confident about working in a team, especially for a project like the 'Cultural Comparison Blog'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new strategy will you use in future group interactions to ensure clear communication and avoid misunderstandings?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of students mediating communication and clarifying project plans? Were the grammar points (second conditional, superlatives, past continuous) naturally integrated into the mediation tasks? How effectively did students use gestures and rephrasing? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation lessons or for reinforcing the specific roles within the 'Cultural Comparison Blog' project? Did the stress and vowel reduction practice contribute to clearer communication?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?" theme and directly applies all learned skills towards the "Cultural Comparison Blog" project, which will be further developed and potentially presented in future units or serves as a culminating activity for this theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>